What is sky130's capacitor matching coefficient, and can we trust it?

The model sizes the unit capacitor from Pelgrom's law, so this coefficient is
what the whole array area rests on. It is not in a datasheet -- it sits inside
the device subcircuits as a mismatch term proportional to 1/sqrt(area), gated
by MC_MM_SWITCH:

    grep -n sw_mm_cmim $PDK_ROOT/$PDK/libs.tech/combined/continuous/models_global.spice
    grep -n carea      $PDK_ROOT/$PDK/libs.tech/combined/continuous/models_capacitors.spice

There is a second set of device models under libs.tech, and a third under
libs.ref. They carry different MiM subcircuits with different mismatch
coefficients, and they disagree. The deck reads the one the PDK's own xschem
setup selects, which is what makes a number from here comparable with a
schematic-driven simulation rather than an independent claim about the process.

Reading an expression is not the same as trusting it, so mim_mc.spice measures
the spread it predicts:

    ngspice -b mim_mc.spice 2>/dev/null | grep -oE 'c = [0-9.e+-]+' | awk '{print $3}' > /tmp/caps.txt
    python3 -c "import statistics as st; v=[float(x) for x in open('/tmp/caps.txt')]; \
      print(f'n={len(v)} mean={st.mean(v)*1e15:.3f} fF sigma/C={100*st.pstdev(v)/st.mean(v):.3f}%')"

The mismatch term falls as 1/sqrt(area), so a device of unit area measures the
coefficient directly -- which is why the deck sizes it that way, and what to
preserve if you resize it. Repeated runs scatter around the coefficient by
roughly the standard error of a spread estimated from that many samples, which
is 1/sqrt(2N) of it, so read several runs rather than one run's digits.

Runs are not reproducible: `set rndseed` does not survive the `reset` that
redraws the device, so ngspice re-seeds itself. Worth knowing before trusting
any analog Monte Carlo in this flow to repeat.

mc_mm_switch defaults to 0 in sky130.lib.spice. The deck sets it to 1. Without
that the spread is exactly zero and the models look like they carry no matching
information at all. mc_pr_switch is the other one, and it is a different
question: process spread moves a whole wafer together, where matching is what
separates two devices sitting side by side.

No schematic and no xschemrc here -- there is nothing to draw. The deck exists
to check one number the entire array sizing rests on.
